AN ITEM SPLIT BY A NESTED LIST IS ONE ITEM.

Three items here: the parent task, the nested note, and the sibling task. The
parent's continuation paragraph is not a fourth. It used to be counted as one.

WHY THE COUNT AND NOT THE BYTES. `format` can only show what the writer does.
This case reads the block model, which is what the writer is given.

Be exact about the harm, because it is easy to overstate. `replace`, `remove`
and `insert-after` take BLOCK indices, and a list is one block however many
items it holds, so a miscount here did NOT shift those indices. What it did do
is report a number that is wrong: `md blocks` told the reader a list had four
items when the author wrote three, and that report is the tool's answer to
"what is in this document".

The count also stands in for the writer's view of the same array. While the
continuation counted as an item, `format` gave it a bullet, and the extra item
then became REAL in the rewritten file. That is where the lasting damage was;
see `format-keeps-a-tail-paragraph-inside-its-item`.

The nested note IS an item and IS counted. Nesting is flattened into one
array, so an item at any depth counts once. Only a continuation does not
count, and `Array.authoredCount` is where that rule lives — use it rather than
`count` whenever the question is how many items the author wrote.

THE LINE RANGE MATTERS AS MUCH AS THE COUNT. L3-9 reaches the last line of the
sibling task, so the continuation is inside the block rather than after it.
That was already right and must stay right.

The model's half of the defect whose writer's half is
`format-keeps-a-tail-paragraph-inside-its-item`. One fix cleared both.
